                                                                   "category":  "Desires",
                                                                   "point":  "The authors argue that clinical algorithms must be rigorously scrutinized to prevent the codification of historical injustices into automated decision-making. They emphasize that when algorithms use race as a proxy for health outcomes, they risk baking systemic inequity into the future of healthcare. From a safetyist perspective, this is a critical alignment failure where the model\u0027s objective function (e.g., predicting risk) inadvertently optimizes for discriminatory outcomes. The document advocates for a proactive, evidence-based approach to auditing these tools before they are widely deployed.",
                                                                   "verbatim":  "When these tools influence high-stakes decisions, whether in the clinic or the courtroom, they propagate inequity into our future.",
                                                                   "excerpt_context":  "Assessment, paragraph 5"
                                                               },
                                                               {
                                                                   "stance":  "aligned",
                                                                   "taxonomy_node_id":  null,
                                                                   "category":  "Intentions",
                                                                   "point":  "The document proposes a three-question framework for evaluating race correction in clinical algorithms: assessing the robustness of evidence, identifying a plausible causal mechanism, and determining if the correction relieves or exacerbates inequities. This approach functions as a safety-oriented governance mechanism, requiring developers to justify the inclusion of sensitive variables. From a safetyist lens, this is a necessary step to prevent \u0027black box\u0027 models from perpetuating harm. The authors note that this requires active participation from professional societies and clinical institutions.",
                                                                   "verbatim":  "When developing or applying clinical algorithms, physicians should ask three questions: Is the need for race correction based on robust evidence and statistical analyses? Is there a plausible causal mechanism for the racial difference that justifies the race correction? And would implementing this race correction relieve or exacerbate health inequities?",
                                                                   "excerpt_context":  "Assessment, paragraph 7"
                                                               }
                                                           ]
                                        },
